\pard\sa80\par
\pard\sb180\sa100\b\f0\fs27 Best small live Codex change\b0\par
\pard\sa70\par
\pard\sa80\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21 Ask Codex to add a deterministic riskBand (low, medium, high) to compareSyntheticCase, return it in the API rule summary, display it in the recommendation metadata, and add one backend assertion. This is safe because it uses synthetic data, introduces no write access, and touches backend, frontend, and automated testing.\par
\pard\sa70\par
\pard\sa80\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21 Suggested prompt:\par
\pard\sa70\par
\pard\sa80\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21 \u8220?In the HR1 repository, add a deterministic riskBand to the synthetic interview workflow: high for any critical difference, medium for noncritical differences, low for no differences. Return it in ruleSummary, render it beside mode/request ID, and add tests. Do not change the model tools, authorization, or deployment. Run the relevant checks.\u8221?\par
\pard\sa70\par
\pard\sa80\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21 Before the interview, practice the change once on a disposable branch. During the interview, narrate: inspect \u8594? state assumption \u8594? modify smallest surface \u8594? test \u8594? review diff.\par
\pard\sa70\par
\pard\sa80\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21 Rollback only the demo change after reviewing the exact paths:\par
\pard\sa70\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\li360\sa20\f1\fs18 git diff -- app.js api/src/interviewCases.js api/test/interviewAssistant.test.js\par
\pard\li360\sa20\f1\fs18 git restore -- app.js api/src/interviewCases.js api/test/interviewAssistant.test.js\par
\pard\sa80\par
\pard\sa70\par
\pard\sa80\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21 Do not run that rollback if those files contain other uncommitted work. The safer interview setup is a clean temporary branch created specifically for the live change.\par
\pard\sa70\par
\pard\sb180\sa100\b\f0\fs27 How to command the room\b0\par
\pard\sa70\par
\pard\li540\fi-240\sa55\f0\fs21 \u8226?\tab Lead every technical detail with its business purpose: \u8220?To reduce manual review, I run rules first.\u8221?\par
\pard\li540\fi-240\sa55\f0\fs21 \u8226?\tab Use precise status words: \u8220?implemented,\u8221? \u8220?configured,\u8221? \u8220?simulated,\u8221? and \u8220?proposed\u8221? are different.\par
\pard\li540\fi-240\sa55\f0\fs21 \u8226?\tab Never apologize for a fixture. Say: \u8220?I designed a deterministic fallback so the demonstration is reliable, and I label it so there is no ambiguity.\u8221?\par
\pard\li540\fi-240\sa55\f0\fs21 \u8226?\tab After showing code, translate it: \u8220?That allowlist means the browser cannot send arbitrary employee or applicant records.\u8221?\par
\pard\li540\fi-240\sa55\f0\fs21 \u8226?\tab If interrupted, answer in this order: direct answer, evidence, tradeoff, next step.\par
\pard\li540\fi-240\sa55\f0\fs21 \u8226?\tab If something fails, keep control: identify the mode badge, use the browser-guided fallback, run the unit test, and explain the failure boundary.\par
\pard\li540\fi-240\sa55\f0\fs21 \u8226?\tab End answers cleanly. Do not fill silence after you have proven the point.\par
\pard\sa70\par
\pard\sa80\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21 Final close:\par
\pard\sa70\par
\pard\sa80\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs21 \u8220?This demonstrates how I coach teams to use AI responsibly: make the business outcome explicit, constrain the model with software, prove the controls with tests, keep the human accountable, and be honest about what remains before production.\u8221?\par
\pard\sa70\par
